WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.400
Ask voters what worries them and you’ll
find jobs rank near the top of most polls.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.520
Politicians often blame trade agreements,
regulations, immigration, or workers in other countries for taking jobs.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.380
They tend to ignore another disruptive force
to the U.S. worker: automation.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.120
Automation has been displacing humans for
decades.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.560
Assembly line workers, switchboard operators,
gas station attendants are just some of those

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.240
whose jobs have disappeared.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.380
More recently, jobs in service and retail
have taken a major hit.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.120
Just think, when was the last time you used
a travel agent to book a trip?

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.120
Or you went to the mall for something you could
buy on your phone?

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.760
For many other types of jobs, the future doesn’t
look much better.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:51.040
Millions of truck drivers, office employees
and fast food workers could be displaced.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.920
But automation is not a zero sum game.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.760
As it eliminates jobs it creates others.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.380
The World Economic Forum estimates 75 million jobs could disappear over the next 4 years.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:08.020
But it also expects automation to create over 130 million new positions.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.920
That’s a net gain of nearly 60 million jobs worldwide.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.140
The challenge is these new jobs require different
skills.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.020
Manual labor will matter less.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.480
Computer programming and coding will matter
more.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.880
Millions of American workers don’t yet have the training to make this transition to a more automated world.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.600
That’s one reason policymakers are discussing
things like tuition-free colleges and money

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.300
for life-time learning programs.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.960
They are pushing for high-speed internet
for every corner of the country.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.360
And they are demanding greater protections
for workers in the gig economy.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.140
America’s history is a story of technological
change and adaptation.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.520
One-hundred years ago, the invention of the assembly line and mass production forced American workers to learn new skills.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.480
To help them, the U.S. government expanded
education.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.680
Businesses provided job-training.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.240
And workers organized in unions to demand
higher wages and fair treatment.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.000
With automation, Americans and America will again need to adapt.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.500
The future of work depends on it.

